  "label": "TMUA 2016 · Paper 2 · Question 5",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2016 · Paper 2 · Question 5 — Count counterexamples below 50 to the claim that numbers 1 or 5 below a multiple of 6 are prime.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Rewrite the implication",
      "body": "<p>The phrase prime if it is 1 or 5 less than a multiple of 6 means: being of that form is claimed to force primality.</p>",
      "recap": "A counterexample has the stated form but is not prime.",
      "pitfall": "A prime outside those residue classes does not test this direction.",
      "pause": "Write the positive numbers below 50 with remainder 1 or 5 modulo 6.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "A if B means B implies A."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Filter the list",
      "body": "<p>The candidates are 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 25, 29, 31, 35, 37, 41, 43, 47, 49. Check which are not prime.</p>",
      "recap": "The hypothesis gives a short finite list.",
      "pitfall": "1 is not prime, even though it is not composite.",
      "pause": "Factor the larger non-prime entries.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l07",
          "reminder": "Check primality against its definition when choosing integer counterexamples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Test primality systematically",
      "body": "<p>For each candidate, test divisibility by primes no larger than its square root; treat one separately using the definition of prime.</p>",
      "recap": "A counterexample must meet the premise but fail primality.",
      "pitfall": "A prime in the list is not a counterexample.",
      "pause": "Mark every non-prime candidate and count them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l07",
          "reminder": "A counterexample must meet the premise but fail primality."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The premise picks numbers congruent to 1 or 5 modulo 6, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math>. Listing them gives 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 25, 29, 31, 35, 37, 41, 43, 47, 49.</p><p>The non-prime entries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>25</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>35</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>7</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The remaining entries are prime. Each listed failure satisfies the premise.</p><p>Thus there are <strong>4 counterexamples: C.</strong> Remember that 1 is neither prime nor composite; non-prime includes it.</p>",
